---
// D429 — bespoke in-kernel reconstruction.
//
// The page IS the recreation: a document sheet centred on the ground, running
// off the bottom edge, ruled by one axis — the running-text column's left edge.
// Masthead and body hang off that axis; a step-smaller note column hangs left
// of it in the margin, each entry on a hanging indent, with the artwork parked
// at the foot of the margin. The mechanic here IS the grid — the source has no
// interaction, so the page is static and the crop at the fold is the point, not
// an accident of viewport height. De-branded: the source masthead becomes the
// work title, its line-drawing doodle becomes a kernel specimen plate, and the
// sheet's yellow ground drops to paper tones (the entry carries no palette, so
// there is nothing to graft and the colour law holds — only --spot marks where
// the artwork sat).
//
// Reference (internal, never shipped): refs/works/d429.jpg, 1600×1000, ground
// L=255. Measured with scripts/ref-geometry.py + per-glyph probes, then
// converted x/1600*100 → cqw. Type is sized by CAP HEIGHT, not by eye: Archivo's
// cap is 0.686em (checked in the woff2, not assumed), so font-size = capH/0.686.
//
//   sheet        x315→1283 (969), centred, top y43   → 60.56cqw col, 2.69cqw top
//   sheet pad    left 43, right 43 (1240→1283)       → padding-inline 2.69cqw
//   note column  x358→626 (268 of the 883 content)   → 268fr
//   gutter       268→658, a 32px wall                → column-gap 3.62%
//   body column  x658→1240 (582) — THE axis          → 583fr
//   masthead     capH 36 (y95→130)                   → 3.28cqw  (36/.686 = 52.5px)
//   sub          capH 19 (y158→176), 10px under it   → 1.73cqw, margin-top .64cqw
//   deep band    sub baseline 176 → body cap top 346 → margin-bottom 9.77cqw
//   body         capH 14, line pitch 25px            → 1.275cqw / line-height 1.225
//   notes        capH 11, line pitch 19.2px          → 1cqw / line-height 1.2
//   note hang    line 1 x358, line 2 x388 (30px)     → 1.9em hanging indent
//   para indent  column 658 → 'Lorem' at 701 (34px)  → text-indent 1.65em
//   lead break   only ONE blank line (y471→522), the → .body__lead margin 1lh;
//                rest of the paragraphs indent only     no gap between the rest
//   figure       ink x366→613, y678→945; notes end   → margin-top 3.08cqw,
//                628, so a 268-square at the foot       aspect-ratio 1
//
// The reference's serif is not ours (the kernel ships two families, neither a
// serif), so the body is Archivo matched on cap height + line pitch — the two
// measurable things — and the glyph-width delta is accepted, per the method.
// The sheet's yellow is likewise the client's, not ours to keep.
//
// Archived case-study copy (removed from DOM 2026-07-17):
//
//   The signature —
//   The page is a sheet, not a screen: one document block centred against the
//   ground and cropped mid-sentence at the fold. Everything obeys a single axis —
//   the masthead and the running serif text share one left edge, while the
//   marginal notes hang to its left, set smaller, each entry indenting its own
//   continuation lines. A deep band of nothing between masthead and text does the
//   pacing; the drawing sits in the margin like a footnote.
//
//   Study notes —
//   The spacing works because the hierarchy is spatial before it is typographic:
//   primary text owns the axis, secondary text hangs off it, and rank is legible
//   from position alone before a single size difference registers. The oversized
//   band under the masthead is the one extravagance, and it is load-bearing — it
//   separates the document's name from the document's body the way a title page
//   separates itself from chapter one. Rebuilding it meant transcribing three
//   measures — note column, gutter, text column — and letting every other
//   decision fall out of them.
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const entry = await getEntry('works', 'd429');
const w = entry!.data;

// The margin column — a second, subordinate voice. Italic entries echo the
// source's asides; the grid never moves for them. Four entries, one italic:
// the reference's own count.
const notes = [
  { text: 'The note column sets a step smaller and hangs every entry from its first line.' },
  { text: 'Asides and citations drop to italic; the measure and the gutter hold.', em: true },
  { text: 'Continuation lines indent, so the margin reads as a ledger of first words.' },
  { text: 'The figure parks at the foot of the margin and never crosses the gutter.' },
];
---

<Base title={`${w.title} — *blue red + purple/`} chrome="work">
  <main class="site">
    <div class="sheet">
      <div class="sheet__grid">
        <header class="masthead">
          <p class="masthead__title ttu">{w.title} Study Doc</p>
          <p class="masthead__sub ttu">Margin note · Running text</p>
        </header>

        <aside class="margin">
          <ul class="margin__notes">
            {notes.map((n) => <li class:list={{ em: n.em }}>{n.text}</li>)}
          </ul>
          <figure class="margin__plate">
            <span class="font-mono">fig. 01</span>
          </figure>
        </aside>

        <div class="body">
          <p class="body__lead">
            <em>One axis rules the sheet,</em> and it belongs to this column. The masthead hangs
            from it, the text hangs from it, and nothing else on the page is allowed an opinion
            about where the left edge sits. What looks like a wide inner margin is the cost of that
            rule — and the page pays it without flinching.
          </p>
          <p>
            The notes opposite are the second voice. They comment, cite, and digress at a smaller
            size, <em>never interrupting the measure of this column,</em> because the gutter between
            the two is a wall, not a suggestion. Each note announces itself with a flush first line
            and tucks the rest behind it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Below the fold the text simply keeps going. The sheet is cropped, not concluded — the
            document grid promises continuity, and the crop is proof that the system runs deeper
            than the viewport. A reader scrolls a document here, not a website, and the grid never
            once breaks character to remind them otherwise.
          </p>
          <p>
            <em>Nothing announces the edge,</em> which is why it convinces. The masthead does not
            sit on a rule, the columns are not boxed, and the note column is separated from the text
            by air alone. Rank arrives as position before it arrives as size, so the page can afford
            to set its second voice only a step down and still be read in the right order — and the
            sentence that runs past the bottom of the screen is
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  /* The rebuilt room, now the whole page. The sheet is centred, starts 43px off
     the top edge, and runs off the bottom — the crop IS the layout, so the page
     is exactly one viewport and clips. Static by design: the source has no
     mechanic, the grid is the mechanic.

     The page is its own query container. This page already declared one on
     .site, and every cqw below sits on a DESCENDANT of it, so the transcription
     survived the promotion intact — but it now resolves against the viewport
     (1600) rather than the old ~1000px stage, which is the whole point. */
  .site {
    container-type: inline-size;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 60.56cqw;
    justify-content: center;
    padding-top: 2.69cqw;
    min-height: 100dvh;
    max-height: 100dvh;
    background-color: var(--paper);
    color: var(--ink);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* Sheet ink starts 43px in on both sides; the bottom border is omitted
     because the reference has no bottom — it is cropped. */
  .sheet {
    padding: 2.69cqw 2.69cqw 0;
    background-color: var(--paper-1, var(--paper));
    border: 1px solid var(--line, var(--ink-2));
    border-bottom: none;
  }

  /* One grid carries the whole sheet: notes 268 / gutter 32 / text 583 of the
     883px content box. The masthead spans only the text column — that is the
     axis rule, stated as geometry rather than as alignment. */
  .sheet__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 268fr 583fr;
    grid-template-areas:
      '.      masthead'
      'margin body';
    column-gap: 3.62%;
    align-items: start;
  }

  .masthead {
    grid-area: masthead;
    margin-bottom: 9.77cqw; /* the deep band between name and body: 156px */
  }
  .masthead__title {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: var(--wght-display, 700);
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3.28cqw, 4.1rem);
    line-height: 1.05;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-display, -0.02em);
  }
  .masthead__sub {
    margin: 0.64cqw 0 0;
    font-size: clamp(0.85rem, 1.73cqw, 2.16rem);
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: 1.2;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    color: var(--ink-1, var(--ink));
  }

  .margin {
    grid-area: margin;
  }
  .margin__notes {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    font-size: clamp(0.75rem, 1cqw, 1.25rem);
    line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--ink-1, var(--ink));
  }
  /* Hanging indent: first line flush on the note axis, continuation lines tucked
     30px behind it. Entries run on one continuous pitch — the reference puts no
     air between them, so the margin reads as a single ledger. */
  .margin__notes li {
    padding-left: 1.9em;
    text-indent: -1.9em;
  }
  .margin__notes li.em {
    font-style: italic;
  }

  /* Where the source parks its line drawing: a de-branded specimen plate at the
     foot of the margin, spanning the column and standing as tall as it is wide
     (the reference's drawing is 268px in a 268px column). The spot ink marks
     "artwork carries colour". */
  .margin__plate {
    margin: 3.08cqw 0 0;
    aspect-ratio: 1;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    background-color: var(--paper);
    border: 1px solid var(--line, var(--ink-2));
  }
  .margin__plate span {
    font-size: clamp(0.6rem, 0.9cqw, 1.1rem);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: var(--spot, var(--ink));
  }

  .body {
    grid-area: body;
    font-size: clamp(0.95rem, 1.275cqw, 1.6rem);
    line-height: 1.225; /* 25px pitch at the reference's 20.4px body */
  }
  .body p {
    margin: 0;
  }
  /* Source rhythm: an italic lead-in opens the block, exactly one blank line
     separates it from the running text, and every paragraph after that is
     indented with no air at all. The gap is one line, so state it as one line. */
  .body__lead {
    margin-bottom: 1lh;
  }
  .body__lead ~ p {
    text-indent: 1.65em;
  }

  /* Phone: the source sheet adapting, not a second design. The two columns are
     the work — collapsing them would break the axis the whole page is about — so
     the sheet widens instead and the note column takes the smallest share that
     still holds a measure. Type is already on rem floors above. */
  @container (max-width: 48rem) {
    .site {
      grid-template-columns: 94cqw;
    }
    .sheet__grid {
      grid-template-columns: 34fr 66fr;
    }
    .masthead {
      margin-bottom: 3.2rem;
    }
    .margin__plate {
      margin-top: 2rem;
    }
  }
</style>
